      "example": {
        "title": "A fee convention changes the smaller gap’s net R",
        "text": "GAPS, 30 shares: 1R $90, gross loss $300, costs $5, net −$305/−3.3889R. At 15 shares, 1R $45, gross loss $150, costs still $5, net −$155/−3.4444R. Gross R stays −3.3333; net R worsens slightly because the flat fee is a larger share of planned risk."
      },
      "exercise": "Recalculate GAPS at 15 rather than 30 shares, including the $5 flat round-trip cost. Give price-risk 1R, notional, gross/net loss, net R, cash after entry and final cash. Explain what changes and what remains constant.",
      "quiz": {
        "question": "GAPS is cut from 30 to 15 shares with the same prices and a flat $5 round-trip fee. Which comparison is correct?",
        "options": [
          "Both gross and net R remain −3.3333 because the share count halves.",
          "Gross R remains −3.3333; net R changes from −3.3889 to −3.4444 because the fixed fee consumes more R.",
          "Net R halves because dollar loss approximately halves."
        ],
        "answer": 1,
        "explanation": "Each denominator halves, as does gross loss, but the $5 cost does not halve.",
        "feedback": [
          "The price multiple stays constant; the fixed fee is not proportional to quantity.",
          "Each denominator halves, as does gross loss, but the $5 cost does not halve.",
          "R divides by the smaller original price-risk denominator, so dollars and multiples respond differently."
        ]
      },
